SPOZ2 0.0

# What is this project supposed to do?
# Humans and AI tools should treat this file as the authoritative
# expression of intent.  Edit it directly or use `spoz2 add ...`.

gist:
    SPOZ2 makes explicit what something is supposed to do, in a form humans and
    machines can understand and maintain as intent evolves. This project is the
    open SPOZ2 file format and the spoz2 command-line tool for creating,
    checking and maintaining SPOZ2 files beside source code.

behaviours:
    - A project can declare its intent in a root file named SPOZ2, kept beside
      its source.
    - Additional or standalone intent can be kept in .spoz2 documents using the
      same format.
    - Humans can read and edit these files directly in any text editor.
    - Software can validate their deterministic structure (spoz2 check) and
      report problems with file and line numbers.
    - Entries can be added from the command line while working (spoz2 add),
      without disturbing the rest of the file.
    - From any subdirectory, the nearest SPOZ2 is found by walking upward (spoz2
      show, check, add, log, diff).
    - Git preserves the evolution of intent alongside the implementation (spoz2
      log, spoz2 diff).
    - spoz2 init drafts the first specification from the codebase's own account
      of itself (README, changelog, file layout): it hands the scaffold plus
      that evidence to a configurable agent command and validates the draft
      before writing it. Without a working agent, init falls back to the plain
      scaffold, loudly, so it always leaves a file. --/agent skips the agent
      deliberately. init also sets up agent entry points by default - AGENTS.md
      always, CLAUDE.md and the skill when the repository shows Claude use -
      with --/agents to opt out and --agents to force all three; trouble there
      is a warning, never a failed init, and init never prompts.
    - A maintainer can make a SPOZ2 discoverable by connecting it to the SPOZ2
      register and submitting evidence (spoz2 register, spoz2 report). Signing
      up comes first, on the web (spoz2.do, email passcode); the CLI guides
      those steps, stores the connection, and submits evidence itself. A
      report carries presence, digest, counts and check outcomes - never the
      text of the SPOZ2. With --github, spoz2 register also sets up GitHub
      Actions: it writes a workflow that installs spoz2 (its runtime cached
      between runs) and runs spoz2 report on every push, so CI evidence
      carries a real syntax check, and it hands the token to the GitHub CLI on
      standard input as the repository secret S2R_TOKEN. It never overwrites a
      different existing workflow without --force, and it leaves committing
      and pushing to the maintainer.
    - A coding agent can fetch everything it needs to follow a SPOZ2 in one
      offline command (spoz2 agent): the canonical adherence protocol, the
      file's format version and byte digest, the inherited Invariant Zero,
      and the specification itself, as delimited text or versioned --json.
      spoz2 agent install writes short managed sections into AGENTS.md (and
      CLAUDE.md with --claude) and, with --skill, the portable skill - all
      generated from the one bundled protocol, never copying the project's
      invariants, preserving surrounding content, idempotent, and refusing
      malformed markers or symlinks that escape the repository. spoz2 agent
      status reports spec validity, binding resolution and integration
      state in wording that never claims an agent read anything or that
      software conforms; --strict turns it into a CI check.
    - Invariants carry stable, plain monotonic numbers ('Invariant 3: ...')
      so humans and agents can refer to them explicitly: Invariant 0 is the
      foundation, project invariants start at 1, spoz2 add invariant numbers
      new entries automatically (explicit numbers respected, duplicates
      refused), spoz2 show invariant 3 resolves a reference, and spoz2 check
      treats a duplicate number as an error. Unnumbered files stay valid.
    - The CLI installs with one curl command: the installer fetches the code,
      fetches the runtime too when it is missing (into the user's home,
      nothing system-wide), links spoz2 onto the PATH and verifies it runs.
      Nobody needs to know or care what language the tool is written in to
      install it.
    - CI builds spoz2 as one standalone file for Linux x86_64, macOS (universal)
      and Windows x64 with a pinned, checksum-verified Raku++ release, proves
      each file runs alone, smoke-tests it, runs the full suite against it on
      Linux and macOS, and keeps the files as build artifacts. Nothing is
      published by that workflow; distribution is a separate decision.

invariants:
    - Invariant 0: humans first. This software exists to help humans
      thrive and respect each person's dignity. It must not cause or assist
      harm to people; no claimed greater good makes a person disposable. It
      must preserve meaningful human oversight: people can understand its
      consequential actions, challenge its decisions, and exercise
      appropriate control, including correction and safe stopping. It must
      honestly represent what it is, what it knows, what it has done, and
      what remains uncertain. No other entry may weaken or override this
      invariant.
    - Invariant 1: The open format and the core CLI must not depend on the
      commercial spoz2.do service; the file belongs to the project.
    - Invariant 2: A SPOZ2 describes intent - what the thing is supposed to
      do - not merely its current implementation.
    - Invariant 3: External standards and policies are referenced, never
      copied into a SPOZ2.
    - Invariant 4: Changing intent is allowed; its history matters and lives
      in Git, not in a separate versioning mechanism.
    - Invariant 5: Deterministic validation (spoz2 check) must not pretend to
      prove semantic quality; 'valid structure' is not 'good product
      thinking'.
    - Invariant 6: Humans and AI must be able to understand the format
      without specialised or proprietary tooling.
    - Invariant 7: The tool never silently overwrites or reformats a user's
      file: init refuses to overwrite, add inserts lines and keeps existing
      text and comments.
    - Invariant 8: The root intent file is named SPOZ2 with no extension;
      further documents use .spoz2; 'spoz2.do' is the product brand and
      never a filename.

constraints:
    - Implemented in straightforward Raku using core modules only; the only
      runtime needs are Rakudo and, for log/diff, Git. Agent-assisted init
      shells out to an external agent command the same way and degrades to the
      scaffold without one.
    - The CLI itself works completely offline: no telemetry, database or cloud
      requirement, and no account needed for any core command. Exactly two
      explicit opt-ins may reach the network: the optional agent command
      behind init, and the register commands (spoz2 register, spoz2 report) a
      maintainer invokes deliberately. Every other command never touches the
      network, and init still works (scaffold) without an agent.
    - Plain terminals and SSH: no TUI and no terminal-UI libraries; terse
      output, no banners.
    - Plain-text, line-based format (not JSON, not YAML) so Git diffs stay clean
      and humans can edit by hand.
    - Unknown sections must be preserved and reported as warnings, not errors,
      so the format can evolve without breaking older files.
    - The CLI must work across operating systems and CPU architectures: the
      tool is pure source, the installer obtains a runtime per platform
      (prebuilt where one exists, built from source with plain warnings
      otherwise), and external tools are reached through portable fallbacks
      (sha256sum, shasum or openssl for digests) - never GNU-only or
      x86-only assumptions.

decisions:
    - 2026-09-12: Invariant numbers are plain monotonic integers (user
      ruling: the dotted 0.0 just adds complexity). The foundation
      invariant is 'Invariant 0'; there is no dotted numbering and no
      reserved 0.x space, and project invariants start at 1. Format 0.0 is
      still in development, so the canonical text and digest changed in
      place (sha256 df43776d2c4d...); the tool still recognises the older
      'Invariant 0.0:' and frozen 'Invariant zero:' leads in existing
      files, which check flags as drift that binds regardless.
    - 2026-09-12: Invariants are numbered so they can be referred to
      explicitly (user ruling). The number lives in the entry text -
      'Invariant 3: ...' - following the Invariant 0.0 convention, so the
      grammar is unchanged and unnumbered files stay valid: check never
      nags them, and numbers appear as entries are added. add assigns the
      next free integer automatically, show invariant N resolves a
      reference, duplicate numbers are check errors because they defeat
      the reference, the agent-drafting prompt asks for numbered
      invariants, and the 0.x space stays reserved for the format's
      foundation. This file's own invariants are now numbered 1 to 8.
    - 2026-09-11: The CLI works across architectures (user ruling). The code
      needed nothing - Raku is architecture-independent - so the work is at
      the edges: the installer falls back from a prebuilt runtime download
      to 'rakubrew build' with honest compiler-and-make prerequisites and a
      named platform, and file digests fall back sha256sum -> shasum ->
      openssl so macOS and the BSDs work without GNU coreutils. Linux and
      macOS are covered by the installer on any architecture rakubrew can
      download or build for; Windows is documented as manual (rakudo.org
      plus the clone-and-symlink steps). Verified on this x86-64 Linux box
      only; other platforms are covered by design and the known-digest
      test, not yet by execution.
    - 2026-09-10: init does a lot automatically by default (user ruling:
      don't make me think - no command-line options to look up). One command
      drafts the spec and installs the agent entry points it detects, safe
      because managed sections preserve surrounding content and are
      idempotent; --/agents opts out, --agents forces everything, and
      nothing ever prompts. This supersedes the same-day choice to prompt
      interactively and require an explicit flag in scripts.
    - 2026-09-10: Practical agent adherence lives in the CLI and stays
      harness-agnostic (agent-adherence brief, plus the user's ruling to
      ship an official portable skill). One canonical protocol is bundled
      in the tool; spoz2 agent prints it in a self-contained packet, and
      every integration is generated from it: thin managed sections that
      point at the command, and the skill, which embeds the protocol so
      the core workflow works by reading the file directly - while stating
      that CLI validation and inherited-binding resolution were skipped.
      Wording is kept honest everywhere: a packet proves nothing was read,
      installed integrations are statements about files, spoz2 check stays
      structural, and evidence of conformance remains the agent's reported
      checks, not a tool claim. Additive only; no invariant weakened, and
      no dependency on any particular harness, model, editor or registry.
    - 2026-09-10: Installation is one curl command (user ruling: much easier,
      and people may not even need to know it uses Raku). The repo ships a
      POSIX-sh installer served raw from GitHub; when no runtime is present
      it fetches Rakudo via rakubrew into the user's home instead of asking
      them to care, writes a launcher into ~/.local/bin, and every failure
      path ends with plain instructions. A branded install URL on a spoz2.do
      domain is a later DNS decision.
    - 2026-09-10: The user ruled the CLI should let a maintainer register a
      SPOZ2 so it is discoverable, signing up first. Sign-up stays on the web
      (register.spoz2.do, email passcode - a CLI cannot read your email);
      spoz2 register guides onboarding and stores the connection: the reports
      URL in .spoz2-register beside the file, the token under ~/.config/spoz2
      and never in the repository. spoz2 report submits the register's
      s2r-report evidence (presence, digest, counts, check outcome) via curl,
      shelled out to like Git. Register errors are advisory - exit 0 unless
      --strict - matching the register's checks-never-block invariant. The
      core stays offline and the open format carries no registry fields.
    - 2026-09-10: The numbering is retro-fitted to start from 0.0 (user
      ruling: version 0.0 is what is being developed, and there are no
      other users yet, so the renumber is safe).  The 0.1 to 0.4 ladder
      of the past two days collapses into the single in-development
      SPOZ2 0.0, whose canonical invariant zero is the HUMANS FIRST
      text; the ladder's history stays in git.  Version immutability
      discipline (frozen text and digest per version, changes only via
      a new version with a public change record) begins with the first
      release.
    - 2026-09-10: Format 0.4, ruled by the user: the headline tightens
      to 'humans first'.  0.3 (published minutes earlier, adopted by no
      file) is superseded, not rewritten: its text and digest stay in
      the record, which is the version rule surviving its first
      temptation.
    - 2026-09-10: Format 0.3, ruled by the user: the designation becomes
      'Invariant 0.0' (retro-fitting the name; the clauses are unchanged
      from 0.2), reserving the 0.x space for the foundation.  The 0.2
      text and digest stay frozen; the tool reads 0.1, 0.2 and 0.3 and
      recognises both leads.
    - 2026-09-10: Format 0.2, ruled by the user: invariant zero gains its
      full canonical wording (purpose, dignity, agency, honesty,
      precedence), published with a digest per version. 0.1 keeps its
      original text and digest; this file moved to 0.2 explicitly. The
      tool now reads both versions, seeds the 0.2 text, verifies the
      binding per declared version, and reports exactly what a check
      verified. Deliberately no inaction clause: invariant zero governs
      what software does, not everything it fails to prevent.
    - 2026-08-23: This project is Customer Zero. SPOZ2's own SPOZ2 is the first
      real use, and the format changes only from what that use teaches.
    - 2026-08-23: Raku, so the author can read and change the tool quickly;
      startup time and portability matter more than cleverness.
    - 2026-08-23: The format is a tiny line-based grammar (header, 'name:'
      sections, indented '- ' entries) rather than JSON/YAML or a DSL;
      continuation lines join with a space, so entries can be wrapped.
    - 2026-08-23: History and diffs come from Git. spoz2 log/diff are thin
      wrappers; there is no internal version control.
    - 2026-08-23: 'decisions' and 'direction' became known sections during
      dogfooding, because there was nowhere to record why choices were made or
      where intent is heading without presenting it as implemented behaviour.
    - 2026-09-06: The user ruled this dialect (SPOZ2 0.1 header, gist/sections,
      git-kept history) is THE SPOZ2 format, superseding the version-bound
      since/until dialect. This tool is now the public reference implementation
      at github.com/nige123/cli.spoz2.do; the earlier dialect's repo was
      removed on 2026-09-07 and survives only as a local git bundle
      (~/archive/app.spoz2.do-prototype.bundle).
    - 2026-09-07: init is agent-assisted by default (ported from the versioned
      prototype) because a scaffold-only init kept surprising the maintainer -
      the point of init is extracting what the project is supposed to do. The
      agent is an external command (SPOZ2_AGENT_CMD, default 'claude -p')
      shelled out to like Git: the CLI itself makes no network calls and still
      works without it.
    - 2026-09-09: The user ruled that every SPOZ2 leads its invariants with
      invariant zero - humans come first - stated positively (help humans
      thrive; never harm one, never help anyone harm one; it wins any
      conflict). init seeds it first in both the scaffold and agent-drafted
      paths; check warns, never errors, when it is missing or not first, so
      existing files stay green while adoption catches up.
    - 2026-09-10: spoz2 register --github (user ruling, alongside the one-paste
      Connect CI on the register): it writes a GitHub Actions workflow that
      installs spoz2 with its runtime cached and runs spoz2 report, which fixes
      the gap where CI reports from the plain reporter carried checks.syntax
      not_run and could never qualify a project for its SPOZ2 badge. The token
      reaches the GitHub CLI on standard input, never argv; committing and
      pushing stay with the maintainer. The sign-up address moved to spoz2.do
      with the register's domain.
    - 2026-09-11: The CLI stays within what both Rakudo and Raku++ run, so it
      can ship as a standalone binary built by Raku++ (user direction: binaries
      for Linux, macOS and Windows). Two Raku++ 3.26 differences were met: (@a,
      @b) := binding flattens both lists into the first array, so marker lines
      unpack into scalars; and a <{...}> regex interpolation in one test became
      starts-with. SPOZ2_TEST_BIN points the suite at a compiled binary, so CI
      tests the executable it ships.
    - 2026-09-11: CI compiles spoz2 with Raku++ --aot, not --bundle. Raku++
      3.26's bundle stub declares the module length as unsigned long, which is
      32-bit on Windows, so MSVC cannot link it and a MinGW build carries no
      modules. --aot parses at build time, avoids the stub, and passes the whole
      suite on Linux.
    - 2026-09-11: How each binary links. Linux links libstdc++ statically, so
      glibc is its only dependency; its floor is glibc 2.38 (Ubuntu 24.04,
      Debian 13, Fedora 39 or newer), set by the Raku++ 3.26 Linux runtime
      itself, so building on an older system cannot lower it. macOS is one file
      holding arm64 and x86_64. Windows uses the MSVC build, which needs only
      system DLLs; a MinGW build is linked fully static or it needs MinGW DLLs
      beside it.
    - 2026-09-11: Windows binaries come from the Raku++ MSVC archive only; its
      file needs only system DLLs. A MinGW file needs MinGW DLLs beside it, and
      linked with -static it crashes at start (Raku++ 3.26). macOS files declare
      macOS 11 as their minimum, matching the Raku++ runtime; left to the
      default they would require the runner's own macOS.

direction:
    - Make a SPOZ2 directly useful to AI coding tools that simply read it;
      later, possibly a command that prints a compact context block for them.
    - The wider spoz2.com/spoz2.do vision (certification, commitments,
      standards intelligence) is out of scope for this CLI and must not leak
      into the open format. The register client commands are the one
      deliberate bridge; the format itself stays registry-free.

